<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

  <!--
    TWO KINDS OF FIXTURE LIVE HERE, both for the same reason: each is markup that must be
    PRECOMPILED INTO A SEPARATE ASSEMBLY to prove anything, and one assembly is cheaper than two.

      Views/Shared/UBookIt/Themes/...   theme views (below)
      Views/Partials/UBookIt/Emails/... email content, at the path a real site would use, so the
                                        renderer is measured discovering a template rather than
                                        being handed one

    A theme, as a theme really is: an ordinary Razor class library whose views are
    precompiled into its own assembly and discovered as an application part. It
    mirrors UBookIt.Web's csproj shape (Sdk.Razor + AddRazorSupportForMvc) so the
    precedence measurement is a fair comparison rather than a comparison between
    two differently-built artefacts.

    IT LIVES UNDER tests/ AND MUST NEVER LIVE UNDER src/UBookIt.Web/Views.
    ViewInventory scans that directory on disk to discover the shipped view set, so
    a fixture placed there would silently join it and every rendering rule would
    start asserting over a test fixture while reporting green.

    Four themes in one assembly, because the theme name is part of the view path
    and a theme is therefore identified by path rather than by assembly:

      complete    all ten required views, at the right paths, with the right models
      partial     two of them, so per-view fallback can be observed
      wrongmodel  one view at a required path declaring a model it never receives
      basemodel   one view declaring a base type the model it receives satisfies
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AddRazorSupportForMvc>true</AddRazorSupportForMvc>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    A theme references the package for its view models, and for the shared partials
    it may call as building blocks. It is referenced BY the test projects and by the
    TestSite — never by UBookIt.Web, which would make it part of the package and
    prove nothing about a separate assembly winning.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\UBookIt.Web\UBookIt.Web.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
